# Normalise line endings on the way into the object database.
#
# `text=auto` lets git decide per file whether the content is text; `eol=lf`
# says that anything it decides *is* text gets LF in the working tree on
# every platform, not just in the repository. Both halves matter here
# because CI runs a Windows leg: without this, a checkout there converts to
# CRLF, and the tree-walking tests that read this repository's own files
# (tests/test_code_standards_scan.py, test_command_depth_scan.py,
# test_removed_command_scan.py, test_skill_retrieval_logging.py) would be
# matching patterns against content whose line endings differ from the
# Linux leg's. Pinning it here means the two legs read byte-identical files.
#
# It also removes a whole class of diff noise: a contributor on Windows
# whose editor writes CRLF can no longer commit a file that shows as
# entirely rewritten.
#
# Binary content is deliberately not listed. `text=auto` already leaves
# anything git detects as binary alone, and the repository's binaries
# (content/rendered/*.pdf, assets/*.png) are either gitignored per-host
# output or already handled by that detection -- adding `-text` rules for
# them would be guarding a case that has not occurred.
* text=auto eol=lf
